# Docker build context of the public repository.
#
# The Dockerfiles build from the root (`context: .`) and copy only `chain/`, `services/` and
# `docker/`. Everything else is dead weight sent to the daemon on every build, and the chain state
# of a running node can weigh gigabytes.
#
# Never exclude `chain/`, `services/` or `docker/`: they are the only sources of the `COPY` steps.
.git
.github
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
**/*.pyo
**/*.log
*.bak

# Runtime-produced state: never a build input, always bulky.
chain-data/
node_data/
ollama-models/
tokenomics/*.json
tokenomics/*.log

# Documentation and web wallet: served as-is, never copied into an image.
docs/
wallet/

# Build-integrity marker of the mirror: it describes the manufacturing, not the product.
.build-complete

# Operator secrets. The whole build context is handed to the Docker daemon and lands in the layer
# cache, whether or not a `COPY` instruction names the file. `deploy/testnet-miner/.env` is created by every operator
# who follows the miner README, and it carries the relay token.
#
# The patterns cover each family of names, not one canonical name: `*.bak` above only matches names
# ending in `.bak`, so a rotation backup such as `.env.bak-20260802-034827` would get through, and
# `*.key` does not match `.webui_secret_key`, which ends in `_key`.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.env.bak*
**/*secret_key*
**/*.secret
**/*.pem
**/*.vrf
# Templates are not secrets and must stay in the context: `**/.env.*` would otherwise exclude them, and an
# exclusion so broad that it quietly breaks a build is barely better than one that is too narrow.
!**/.env.example
!**/*.env.example
